;;; *forbid* misses a refutation resting on an `absent` over a relation only
;;; a plan a node compiles writes — M1e2 **W17**, register row
;;; `W17/forbid-chain-node-writes` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), found by
;;; S1e2.2.17 (2026-11-04) probing the witness's mechanism.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `chk` concludes `(false)` from `(p ?y)` while `(x ?y)` is absent. Only
;;; `q2`'s plan writes `x`, and it is compiled from the activator `(q2 a)`,
;;; which `mk2` derives from `(p2 a)`. Root holds neither candidate, so it
;;; compiles no plan of `q2` and derives no `x`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `{(p a)}` dies on `(absent (x a))`, and `{(p a), (p2 a)}` lives, `q2`'s
;;; plan deriving `(x a)` there: a dead hole. `x` is a relation a commitment
;;; can extend, through the activator a commitment derives, so this is
;;; *forbid*'s `direct` case (the user's ruling of 2026-11-02): the program
;;; is refused at every command that takes the universe.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `3efd3f5f` it loads: R three states, the one dead hole {(p a)}, exit
;;; 0, in every mode and `ein oracle`. *forbid*'s *extendable* is D, and `x`
;;; is not in D: D's closure follows the plans root compiled, and only a plan
;;; root never compiled writes `x`. The static check calls `chk`'s read of
;;; `x` static, and flags the program for `q2`'s activator site alone. The
;;; status line names no site.

(relation seed T) (relation p T) (relation p2 T) (relation q2 T) (relation x T)

(seed a)

(hrule g  () :match (seed ?x) :assert (p ?x))
(hrule g2 () :match (seed ?x) :assert (p2 ?x))

(rule mk2 ()    :match (p2 ?x) :assert (q2 ?x))
(rule q2  (?n)  :match (seed ?n) :assert (x ?n))
(rule chk ()    :match (and (p ?y) (absent (x ?y))) :assert (false))
